INT. PET CEMETERY CHAPEL - DAY

Folding chairs, silk flowers, a tiny coffin the size of a shoebox on a velvet-draped table. Organ music from a small speaker.

REVEREND CLEMENTINE "CLEM" OKONKWO-PRATT (60s, vestments, a voice built for sermons) stands behind the lectern. In the front row sits WINSTON "WINNIE" BARNABY-LEE (50s, ill-fitting black suit), clutching a handkerchief. Beside him sits his sister AGATHA "AGGIE" BARNABY-LEE (50s, enormous hat, a casserole dish in her lap).

CLEM
We are gathered here today to honor the life of Biscuit.

WINNIE
(sobbing)
Biscuit.

AGGIE
(sobbing)
Biscuit.

CLEM
Biscuit was a hamster of rare character.

WINNIE
She was!

AGGIE
^He was!

Clem pauses. Looks at both. Winnie blinks at Aggie.

WINNIE
She was a she.

AGGIE
He was a he. I raised him.

WINNIE
I bought her at the pet store. She had the little pink nose.

AGGIE
Winston, that was Biscuit's cousin.

CLEM
Mr. and Ms. Barnaby-Lee. Please.

She raises both hands. They subside. She clears her throat.

CLEM (CONT'D)
Whether Biscuit was male, female, or the subject of a clerical error, we honor the hamster.

WINNIE
Amen.

AGGIE
Amen.

CLEM
Biscuit loved sunflower seeds and the wheel. She ran it for hours.

AGGIE
He ran it for days.

WINNIE
He never!

AGGIE
You just said "he."

WINNIE
Slip of the tongue.

CLEM
Shall we sing?

Organ swells. Winnie and Aggie sing, badly, in two different keys. Clem winces but does not stop them.

At the end, Aggie lifts her casserole dish and sets it on the coffin.

AGGIE
Tuna noodle. Biscuit's favorite.

WINNIE
(softly)
Hamsters can't eat tuna.

AGGIE
Well, he can now.

Clem closes her eyes, and, for the first time, smiles.
